#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198078 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f49ef1c2-06de-597c-bf4d-6d613989987e" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Set Using a Conditional Definition"}] :question/id #uuid "cfd5a7b6-4ee8-5c66-9b1d-81d1d9a1aee7" :question/math-academy-id "e-13144" :question/problem "Describe the set $S = \\{-9,-8,-7,..., 5\\}$ using a conditional definition." :question/worked-solution "The set $S$ contains all integer numbers between $-9$ and $5$ (including $-9$ and $5$).\n\nTherefore, the set can be written as\n\n$$\nS = \\{x \\in \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\mathbb Z} \\:: \\: \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}-9} \\leq x \\leq \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}5} \\}\n$$\n\n**Note**: This definition is not unique. For example, we can write $S$ equivalently as follows:\n\n$$\nS = \\big\\{x \\in \\mathbb Z \\:: \\: -10 \\lt x \\lt 6 \\big\\}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198092 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9df722a8-2db6-5bb8-a69c-b81b33037ab2" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Given Set Using a Geometric Sequence"}] :question/id #uuid "e81a9a3f-3dbb-5244-8ea0-9b967bb5a46f" :question/math-academy-id "e-13184" :question/problem "Describe the set $S=\\left\\{\\ldots, -36, -6, -1, -\\dfrac1{6}, -\\dfrac1{36},...\\right\\}$ using a constructive definition.\n\n*Hint: Take $a_1 = -\\dfrac16$ in the sequence that generates the elements of $S$.*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the elements of our set\n\n$$\n\\ldots,\\quad -36, \\quad -6, \\quad -1, \\quad -\\dfrac1{6}, \\quad -\\dfrac1{36}, \\quad \\ldots\n$$\n\nform a two-way infinite geometric sequence with a common ratio\n\n$$\nr=\\dfrac16{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr &= \\frac{1}{6} \\\\\n&= (\\frac{-6}{-36}) \\\\\n&= (\\frac{-1}{-6}) \\\\\n&= ((-\\frac{1}{6}))/((-1)) \\\\\n&= ⋯\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting\n\n$$\na_1=-\\dfrac16\n$$\n\nthe $n$th term $a_n$ of our sequence for $n\\in\\mathbb{Z}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= a_{1} \\cdot r^{n - 1} \\\\\n&= (-\\frac{1}{6}) \\cdot (\\frac{1}{6})^{n - 1} \\\\\n&= - (\\frac{1}{6}) \\cdot (\\frac{1}{6})^{-1} \\cdot (\\frac{1}{6})^{n} \\\\\n&= - (\\frac{1}{6})^{n}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can define our set using a constructive definition as follows:\n\n$$\nS = \\left\\{-\\left(\\dfrac16\\right)^{n} \\:: \\: n\\in \\mathbb{Z}\\right\\}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198093 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ad21350b-2e3b-57ee-a28c-32a7bd1b2513" :knowledge-point/title "Defining a Set Conditionally Using the Roots of a Polynomial"}] :question/id #uuid "a67f56bb-352b-5126-84c8-c38fc3d0532c" :question/math-academy-id "e-13185" :question/problem "Find the missing coefficients $p$ and $q$ in the constructive definition of the set given below.\n\n$\\{\\pm 3\\textrm{i}\\} = \\Big\\{z \\in \\mathbb{C} \\:: \\: 4z^2+pz+q = 0 \\Big\\}$" :question/worked-solution "The given set contains the roots $z\\in\\mathbb C$ of some quadratic polynomial $f(z)$.\n\nSince the quadratic polynomial has roots\n\n$$\nz_1=-3\\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nz_2=3\\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nand the coefficient of the quadratic term is $a=4$, we can write the polynomial as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(z) &= a(z - z_{1})(z - z_{2}) \\\\\n&= 4 \\cdot (z - (-3\\textrm{i}))(z - (3\\textrm{i})) \\\\\n&= 4 \\cdot (z + 3\\textrm{i})(z - 3\\textrm{i}) \\\\\n&= 4 \\cdot (z^{2} - (3\\textrm{i})^{2}) \\\\\n&= 4 \\cdot (z^{2} - 9\\textrm{i}^{2}) \\\\\n&= 4 \\cdot (z^{2} + 9) \\\\\n&= 4z^{2} + 36\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the given set as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{\\pm 3\\textrm{i}\\} = \\Big\\{z \\in \\mathbb{C} \\:: \\: 4z^2+\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0}z+\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}36} = 0 \\Big\\}\n$$\n\nFinally, $p=0$ and $q=36$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198094 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1c5b3eb6-187f-5f16-a32e-66b0842adfe9" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Given Set Using an Arithmetic Sequence"}] :question/id #uuid "231da77b-8370-543a-abf0-effb78e50802" :question/math-academy-id "e-13186" :question/problem "Describe the set $S=\\{..., -5, -3, -1, 1, 3,...\\}$ using a constructive definition.\n\n*Hint: Take $a_1 = 1$ in the sequence that generates the elements of $S$.*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the elements of our set\n$\\ldots, \\quad -5, \\quad -3, \\quad -1, \\quad 1, \\quad 3, \\quad \\ldots$\nform a two-way infinite arithmetic sequence with common difference $d=2{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd &= 2 \\\\\n&=-3 - (-5) \\\\\n&=-1 - (-3) \\\\\n&= 1 - (-1) \\\\\n&= ⋯\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting $a_1=1$, the $n$th term $a_n$ of our sequence for $n\\in\\mathbb{Z}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= a_{1} + (n - 1) \\cdot d \\\\\n&= 1 + (n - 1) \\cdot 2 \\\\\n&= 1 + 2n - 2 \\\\\n&= 2n - 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can define our set using a constructive definition as follows:\n\n$$\nS = \\big\\{2n-1 \\:: \\: n \\in \\mathbb{Z}\\big\\}\n$$\n\n**Watch Out!**\n\nThe constructive definition of a set is typically not unique.\n\nSince the elements of $A$ are generated by a *two-way* infinite arithmetic sequence, we could have picked *any* term in our sequence as $a_1$ when determining $a_n$. We selected $a_1 = 1$ because this led to a nice, simple expression in our constructive definition."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198716 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ad21350b-2e3b-57ee-a28c-32a7bd1b2513" :knowledge-point/title "Defining a Set Conditionally Using the Roots of a Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "0fe06d7e-3cd9-5288-8190-6f570dd805db" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195307} {:answer-field/id #uuid "13610ce8-a13d-5b0d-adbd-be1f7f021e9f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195308}] :question/id #uuid "daeebfae-6abc-50c7-b86a-29781d3e20e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-181608" :question/problem "Find the missing coefficients in the constructive definition of the set ${3, 4}$ given below.${3, 4} = {n ∈ Z: 2n^{2} -$\n{{field-1}}\n$n +$\n{{field-2}}\n$= 0}$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198721 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ad21350b-2e3b-57ee-a28c-32a7bd1b2513" :knowledge-point/title "Defining a Set Conditionally Using the Roots of a Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "81ec4ac7-c58c-54fd-b25d-7a69d7cee835" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195342} {:answer-field/id #uuid "d1d65a68-556e-5cd7-a21b-c05da35d7021" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195343}] :question/id #uuid "3f84e8c3-0aa3-5680-ba44-b0d4f51527fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-181695" :question/problem "Find the missing coefficients in the constructive definition of the set ${-1, \\frac{1}{3}}$ given below.${-1, \\frac{1}{3}} = {q ∈ Q: 3q^{2} +$\n{{field-1}}\n$q -$\n{{field-2}}\n$= 0}$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198771 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9df722a8-2db6-5bb8-a69c-b81b33037ab2" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Given Set Using a Geometric Sequence"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab9b0b3f-b15e-5055-8391-6a00a8a6ab80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1)/(3) ⋅ 2^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195939} {:answer/id #uuid "cbfa0d86-cf70-5f60-9429-04161e6de2e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2)/(3) ⋅ 3^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195940} {:answer/id #uuid "efe290b7-6353-5af9-b4bf-685865ed0034" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1)/(2) ⋅ 3^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195941} {:answer/id #uuid "2c33a4ce-b38e-5507-b4b4-59c57b0f378d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3 ⋅ 2^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195942} {:answer/id #uuid "6547d7f2-2d9b-5294-8559-a3673ef200d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195943}] :answer-field/id #uuid "df590f96-bdbc-5cea-924f-b7889883e3c2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195938} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d01f687-8a0c-58a4-9d67-1d16caba6c6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "N" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195945} {:answer/id #uuid "3aeb5059-9e9d-5377-ba3f-e238cb0bd3af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195946} {:answer/id #uuid "665b400c-0bbe-5234-8b41-b385f197622c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195947}] :answer-field/id #uuid "a837f807-51e1-5d8a-a302-8e17816924de" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195944}] :question/id #uuid "4a35af51-a0e2-50d0-bbd3-1c0dc3bc52aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-183328" :question/problem "Describe the set $S = {…, 3, 6, 12, 24, 48,...}$ using a constructive definition.\n\n*Hint: Take $a_{1} = 6$ in the sequence that generates the elements of $S$.*\n\n$2^{n}$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}}"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198799 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1c5b3eb6-187f-5f16-a32e-66b0842adfe9" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Given Set Using an Arithmetic Sequence"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83b7f204-b76a-5011-80df-0ef738d52df5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186196319} {:answer/id #uuid "39267901-ead7-535b-a1b4-118ce458df1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186196320} {:answer/id #uuid "55414efd-cbc6-58f4-a67e-ed2154f8565f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2n + 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186196321} {:answer/id #uuid "791b55ad-2642-597b-902b-6c7d58488cf5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2n + 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186196322}] :answer-field/id #uuid "0a08c691-e074-5bef-ab8c-6e4b5b3bc776" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186196318} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e09f4c73-e999-5fc4-9b8f-ff6b32a7a665" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "is odd" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186196324} {:answer/id #uuid "e29a79d4-aeaf-53f7-8e41-8e9dbfd475e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "is even" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186196325} {:answer/id #uuid "e6736b06-1a2a-5e25-ac6e-ce4d34b2fa7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∈ Z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186196326} {:answer/id #uuid "17219dcb-5a4d-5930-8b43-46f9ac1f95e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∈ Q" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186196327} {:answer/id #uuid "1a7ed4d4-ad27-5fb2-8900-39b57c9dc562" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∈ N" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186196328}] :answer-field/id #uuid "a3005cbd-3e29-52d4-b866-75bd8dfcc436" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186196323}] :question/id #uuid "1160b89b-a1bd-57ab-af47-94edb968dd50" :question/math-academy-id "q-183764" :question/problem "Describe the set $S = {...,-4,-2, 0, 2, 4,...}$ using a constructive definition.\n\n*Hint: Take $a_{1} = 2$ in the sequence that generates the elements of $S$.*\n\n$2n$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}}"}]}
